{"title":"2015 CIMC Problems/Part A Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_CIMC_Problems/Part_A_Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Determine the <b>largest</b> positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/d/b7da4e63ed7f853d5e4ac1c68744a30f402e7c89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n &lt; 500$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"64\" height=\"15\" /> for which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/6/cc65f268e03f7bf47fc0e617cdf18d50f115e4b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6048(28^{n})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"80\" height=\"20\" /> is a perfect cube (that is, it is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/6/696b99e5b1c1a2fcfcfa08c9802dfba947e33cc6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m^{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"25\" height=\"17\" /> for some positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" />).\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_CIMC_Problems/Part_A_Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For an integer to be a perfect cube, the powers in its prime factorization must all be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, meaning we need to find the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/6/cc65f268e03f7bf47fc0e617cdf18d50f115e4b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6048(28^{n})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"80\" height=\"20\" /> and then see for which values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> the powers of the prime factorization are divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/f/26f7b0fbfd581296912f48842f79e98b52eac73a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$28^{n} = 4^{n} \\times 7^{n} = 2^{2n} \\times 7^{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"201\" height=\"18\" />, so we want to focus on the powers of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/8/c38ea3243593e062ee7861d17121103f256f06f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6048$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> as well. We can see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/8/c38ea3243593e062ee7861d17121103f256f06f6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6048$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/c/bcc0f9f13e3648d2061612c4f4739df3d161a9ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{5} \\times 7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />, so the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/6/cc65f268e03f7bf47fc0e617cdf18d50f115e4b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6048(28^{n})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"80\" height=\"20\" /> contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/6/7967fb628bcb06a5d0f31e807aedfcf1f05a4f1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2n + 5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/3/3432c02aafb7ed1950ab40e9a9e841981bebe400.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7^{n + 1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>Using modular arithmetic on either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/0/d0081a0583ea3a6edcf8e5fd5ad660b55fb52264.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n + 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/5/ac5429dc4aa7ba1f3b20be4599a34c4220cc79db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> more than a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. The largest possible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/9/a39b14023c98c943554d150561aacc188c4ce26d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$500$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/4/eb44dca4687c902cde44c7dd7fc25f30ecf9528d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{497}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"40\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_CIMC\" title=\"2015 CIMC\">2015 CIMC</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_CIMC_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2015 CIMC Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_CIMC_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2015 CIMC Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_CIMC_Problems/Part_A_Problem_4\" title=\"2015 CIMC Problems/Part A Problem 4\">Part A Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_CIMC_Problems/Part_A_Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2015 CIMC Problems/Part A Problem 6 (page does not exist)\">Part A Problem 6</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" styl